A||N||5||Bill Shenefelt||Clutch Fork wear||||1||
Z||000000||Bill Shenefelt||11-20-2001||02:07 PM||||I am new to the list and did a search so I hope I am not just asking an old question, but here goes.  I have an 86 fiero se v6 with a 4 speed.  I had the clutch replaced and had disengagement problems.  Was the arm to the slave cylinder.  Arm was replaced sith a "solid metal(rather than the 1/8 inch sheet metal type) .  Wore out the fork in a few thousand miles.  Replaced clutch, fork and throwout bearing.  did it again.  The end of the fork wears or deforms and then the clutch disengages the whole way to the floor(until it just wont anymore!)<BR>Is there a difference in forks, external arms or something that is causing the fork to wear out.  I do not "ride" the clutch and 4 or 5 thousand miles per fork is silly and expensive.<BR>If someone has had similar troubles and fixed the problem, please email me or somehow let me know what I am doing wrong.<BR>Bill Shenefelt shene@killi.net<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||11-20-2001||06:32 PM||||Hello Bill, welcome to the forum!<P>I searched "archive TD&Q" for "clutch fork wear" and came up with some good info.  Here's one....http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010418-2-005892.html<P>It could be your slave isn't fully releasing for some reason.  In any event, you came to the right place to get technical info!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-20-2001||06:51 PM||||Fork wearing at that rate is likely one or two things.<P>there is something binding.<BR>it wasn't installed (lubed) right.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPAW||11-21-2001||10:05 AM||||Bill,<P>I know the cast arm from the 84 4sp transmission is shorter that the fabricated release arm from the 86 5spd - 4cyl.<BR>If the 86 4spd - v6 uses the same arm as the 5 spd and this was swapped with an arm from an 84 4spd it would result in a substantial amount of overtravel in the clutch fork when the pedal is pressed to the floor- possibly causing it to bend or bind up the throwout bearing.<BR>If someone could measure the length of the arm from an 86 v6-4spd you could compare to yours and maybe eliminate this possiblity.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bill Shenefelt||11-21-2001||10:30 AM||||Thanks Paul.  Initially it was in an 86 4 speed and then I had to change out the transmission to another 4 speed, an 85 I think.  I did reuse the arm from the initial changeout(solid arm) so maybe that is the problem. I have the arm that came with the 4 speed I got as a replacement so I'll try that arm when I change out the fork again.<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||11-21-2001||11:16 AM||||Be sure that the Throw Out bearing slides FREELY on the input shaft. You should be able to slide a piece of writing paper twixt the bearing and the shaft.||||reg||1
